issue='The exponent is recovered as k−ε for a positive constant ε, and n is the total number of vertices in the incidence graph. The source asks for an optimal approximation tradeoff but does not fix a complete benchmark convention: whether k is the actual optimum controlling an algorithm’s running time or a supplied target with a promise; whether the displayed n^{k−ε} budget permits an arbitrary computable multiplier f(k) as its lower-bound theorems do; the joint asymptotic regime and desired precision in k and ε; and whether matching lower bounds are required under SETH or unconditionally. The nearby candidate (ln n)^{1/ρ(k)} does not specify ρ or its quantifiers and cannot replace the full tradeoff. These choices affect the answer and are not invented here.'
notes=['Restored the missing positive constant ε and the incidence-vertex size parameter n.','Kept the question as an optimal approximation tradeoff rather than converting the nearby unspecified ρ(k) expression into a binary conjecture.','Distinguished ordinary approximation by OPT from the source’s absolute k-versus-h promise gap; the multiplicative ratio associated with that gap is h/k.','Recorded the parameterized running-time and promise conventions that remain unspecified in the concluding question.','Checked the revised full source and the 9 September 2026 follow-up, which still describes substantial gaps for Set Cover.']
save(i,dict(title='Approximation tradeoff for Set Cover below exhaustive search',formal='Determine the best approximation ratio for parameterized Set Cover achievable in time n^{k−ε}, where ε is a positive constant and k refers to the solution-size parameter. The missing exponent in the extraction is k−ε, and n counts all vertices of the bipartite set–element incidence graph. The source contrasts its known logarithmic-power hardness with a polynomial-time logarithmic approximation and asks for the intervening tradeoff. A completed benchmark still needs the precise parameter/promise convention, allowable f(k) multiplier, asymptotic precision and lower-bound hypothesis stated below; this card does not silently choose them.',definitions='An instance is a finite bipartite graph I=(S⊔U,E), with one vertex for each available set in S and each universe element in U. An edge (s,u) means set s contains u. All vertices, sides and incidences are explicit; n=|S|+|U|, not just |U| or the number of encoded bits. For nonempty U with every element incident to some set, OPT(I)=min{|C|:C⊆S and every u∈U has a neighbor in C}. A cover is an explicit subset C, and its ordinary multiplicative approximation ratio is |C|/OPT(I). Empty or uncoverable instances can be recognized separately; the meaningful ratio regime has 1≤OPT(I)≤|S|. In the source’s ordinary approximation definition, an algorithm must return a valid cover with |C|≤r·OPT(I) on every coverable input. Its parameterized lower bounds instead take an integer target k and distinguish OPT(I)≤k from OPT(I)>h for an absolute threshold h>k, leaving the middle range unrestricted. That promise gap corresponds to a multiplicative scale h/k, not h itself. The theorem permits running time f(k)n^{k−ε} for an arbitrary computable function f, whereas the concluding question displays n^{k−ε}. The choice between actual-optimum-dependent running time and a supplied target with a promise has to be recorded explicitly before optimizing a ratio. The intended time improvement uses fixed ε>0, independent of n; it is not a saving that vanishes as n grows. It is also different from n^{o(k)} or fixed-parameter time f(k)n^c with c independent of k. The source’s SETH is the deterministic Strong Exponential Time Hypothesis: for every η>0 there is an integer clause width q such that q-CNF satisfiability on N variables has no deterministic O(2^{(1−η)N}poly(M))-time algorithm, where M is the explicit formula size. This is a hypothesis used for the cited lower bound, not a proven fact or an automatically chosen hypothesis for the unfinished optimality target. Natural logarithms are used in the displayed approximation scales, and logarithmic asymptotics concern sufficiently large n.',question_type='asymptotic_complexity',answer_criterion='Specify the exact solution-size promise, input and running-time model, permitted parameter factors and asymptotic comparison of approximation ratios. Then characterize the achievable ratio with matching upper and lower bounds at that precision, with every conditional lower-bound assumption explicit. A single improved factor, a hardness result in a different time regime, or the unspecified nearby function (ln n)^{1/ρ(k)} is not the requested full tradeoff. The statement remains incomplete until those source-supported conventions are fixed.',context_blocks=[dict(title='The known upper benchmark is logarithmic',text='The source recalls a polynomial-time algorithm returning at most (1+ln n)OPT sets. Its question asks whether allowing running time close to n^k can improve the approximation scale substantially.',citation='primary'),dict(title='The source theorem is an absolute optimum gap',text='For ε,δ∈(0,1) and sufficiently large k, Theorem 1 rules out f(k)N^{k−ε}-time distinction between OPT≤k and OPT>H under SETH, where H=(1/(1+δ))(log N/log log N)^{1/k}. The corresponding approximation threshold is H/k. Fixed-k summaries may absorb factors of k; a full joint tradeoff cannot silently do so.',citation='primary'),dict(title='The displayed candidate does not define the whole problem',text='The conclusion mentions ratios of the form (ln n)^{1/ρ(k)}, then asks for the best possible ratio. It gives no particular function ρ or quantifiers selecting that subfamily as the benchmark target.',citation='full'),dict(title='The gap remains visible in September 2026',text='The recent MMSA paper reviews Set Cover’s logarithmic algorithm and logarithmic-power parameterized hardness, and explicitly asks whether the gap can be closed. Its new results on deeper monotone-circuit levels do not determine this n^{k−ε} Set Cover tradeoff.',citation='mmsa')],why='The intended curve would describe how approximation quality changes when an algorithm can spend nearly the time needed for exhaustive parameterized search, a basic unresolved tradeoff for a central covering problem.',references=refs,progress=[dict(date='2019',text='A gap-producing reduction strengthens conditional parameterized Set Cover hardness and leaves the optimal n^{k−ε}-time approximation ratio open.',citation='primary'),dict(date='2026-09-09',text='The APPROX paper reviews the remaining logarithmic-versus-logarithmic-power gap for Set Cover while advancing related monotone satisfying-assignment problems.',citation='mmsa')],status='uncertain',status_note='Read the source’s instance and approximation definitions, exact Theorem 1 threshold, conclusion and April 2019 full revision. The 9 September 2026 paper confirms that substantial Set Cover approximation gaps remain. That evidence does not fix every parameter and acceptance convention for this broad optimal-tradeoff question; these are recorded as the remaining formulation issue.',model_self_contained=False,requires_context=True,statement_review=dict(status='needs_specification',reviewed_on=DATE,notes=notes,remaining_issue=issue),review_note=issue,importance=dict(score=c['importance']['score'],method='editorial',reason='A central approximation-versus-time question for parameterized covering, with a continuing gap documented in September 2026. A precise joint regime is needed to compare the results. Existing score retained.',assessed_on=DATE),working_summary=dict(sentences=['Set Cover seeks the fewest set vertices covering all element vertices in an explicit incidence graph.','The source asks for the optimal approximation ratio in time n^{k−ε}, with a fixed positive exponent saving.','Known conditional lower bounds leave a gap below the polynomial-time logarithmic approximation.','A September 2026 follow-up still describes that gap, while obtaining results for related monotone-circuit problems.','The card recovers the missing exponent but leaves the parameter promise, allowed f(k) factor and exact optimality criterion to be specified.'],basis='saved_sources',source_basis='source-text',written_on=DATE,source_refs=['primary','full','mmsa'])),notes=notes,checked_sources=[r['url'] for r in refs],state='improved_unfinished')
